WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.669
Speaker 1: OK, from the standpoint of jobs
and unemployment, things are continuing to

00:00:05.669 --> 00:00:06.669
improve.

00:00:06.669 --> 00:00:13.230
And I am further reassured that what I told
you yesterday would happen by September, October,

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:14.400
November is going to.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:19.490
And all of these right wingers, as I told
you yesterday, are very, very worried about

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:26.170
how it's more profitable to be on unemployment
than to go back and work jobs at restaurants

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:30.230
and retail stores and other places that are
now reopening as we exit the pandemic.

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:37.030
My position on this has been the enhanced
unemployment has been one of the most effective

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:39.400
pieces of covid stimulus.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:40.400
Sure.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:44.100
Anecdotally, there are some people who are
making roughly the same or maybe even a little

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:45.530
bit more with that enhanced unemployment.

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:49.950
And maybe they're choosing not to go back
to work for the time being, but that unemployment

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:53.470
is going to end and they're going to have
no choice but to go back to work.

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:56.620
We are starting to see this increasingly happen.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:58.760
And we now have a new report from Yahoo!

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:04.320
Finance based on new Department of Labor data,
initial filings last week.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.680
We just we learned today on Thursday, we get
the numbers.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:14.180
For the previous week, new jobless claims
have dropped below 400000 for the first time

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:16.650
since March of twenty twenty.

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:20.640
This is a fifth consecutive week with a new
low.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.910
This is now a 14 month low.

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:28.430
Fewer and fewer Americans are becoming newly
unemployed.

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:34.400
Initial jobless claims came in at three hundred
and eighty five thousand just below the expected

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.180
three hundred and eighty seven thousand.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:45.110
Continuing claims are at three point seventy
seven million, slightly higher than the three

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:48.220
point six million that were expected.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:54.200
Again, this is the first time since the start
of the pandemic where new jobless claims have

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.960
dropped below 400000.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.000
And this is now starting to get close to the
pre pandemic weekly rate.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.130
This is an important thing to understand.

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:04.770
This is not a political statement.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:08.599
This is just what it is in a good, normal
economy.

00:02:08.599 --> 00:02:11.469
You still have new unemployment claims every
week.

00:02:11.469 --> 00:02:15.989
And, you know, we've talked before about at
what point does the risk of going out in the

00:02:15.989 --> 00:02:21.489
world get back to the baseline level because
it was never zero even before covid you could

00:02:21.489 --> 00:02:25.480
go out and catch the flu, you could catch
a stomach bug, you could get run over by a

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:27.099
bus, et cetera.

00:02:27.099 --> 00:02:33.489
So it's about going back to the baseline we
were seeing even in the great economy that

00:02:33.489 --> 00:02:39.299
I agree was was in place during the Trump
administration before the pandemic hit.

00:02:39.299 --> 00:02:40.299
Great in many ways.

00:02:40.299 --> 00:02:43.120
And yet we still had stagnant wages and other
issues.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:49.309
You still saw 200 something new, 200 something
thousand new unemployment claims every week.

00:02:49.309 --> 00:02:54.739
So when you look at that number three eighty
five, it's almost back down to the pre pandemic

00:02:54.739 --> 00:02:55.919
level.

00:02:55.919 --> 00:03:02.519
This is further evidence that as people's
unemployment benefits continue to start to

00:03:02.519 --> 00:03:09.189
and continue to run out and as more and more
aspects of the economy are reopened, as more

00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:13.219
and more people's kids go back to school so
that parents who don't have child care can

00:03:13.219 --> 00:03:19.019
actually get back to work, we are going to
see the issue that the right claims is a big

00:03:19.019 --> 00:03:23.760
issue of people just hanging out at home,
raking in the dough on unemployment.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:24.950
It's going to resolve itself.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:26.120
The unemployment is going to end.

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:28.340
The enhanced unemployment ends in September.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:31.949
People state unemployment will ultimately
run out even with the various extensions that

00:03:31.949 --> 00:03:33.579
different states provide.

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:35.200
And people are going to have to go back to
work.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:41.159
Now, there's going to be a real question here
about whether we see stronger lobbying efforts

00:03:41.159 --> 00:03:42.159
for better wages.

00:03:42.159 --> 00:03:47.430
And, you know, the economy is a complicated
thing when we say, what about a 15 dollar

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:48.430
minimum wage?

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:51.999
As I said before, there are parts of the country
where cost of living is so high that a 15

00:03:51.999 --> 00:03:53.790
dollar minimum wage is too low.

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:55.079
That's just the reality.

00:03:55.079 --> 00:04:00.629
There are other parts of the country where
the cost of living is very, very low.

00:04:00.629 --> 00:04:05.729
And going to a 15 dollar minimum wage immediately
actually could be a significant shock to the

00:04:05.729 --> 00:04:11.049
economy, particularly states that still have
the federal minimum wage as the prevailing

00:04:11.049 --> 00:04:12.049
minimum wage.

00:04:12.049 --> 00:04:17.030
And so these are really regionalized issues,
much the same way depending on which state

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:21.359
you live in and depending on what the cost
of living is and depending on what the wages

00:04:21.359 --> 00:04:26.340
are that are being paid there, there are jobs
that, as a result of all of this, are probably

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:30.169
going to have to start paying people more
in order to get people to come back to work.

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:34.390
Part of that is going to be because of some
large businesses, everything from, you know,

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:35.390
Amazon.

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:39.080
Bank of America has made an announcement,
a similar announcement have upped the minimum

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:40.729
wage that they are going to pay.

00:04:40.729 --> 00:04:45.400
This is going to lead to some businesses having
to pay more in order to attract people.

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:46.430
And that's OK.

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:50.000
Now, if you said they're going to have to
pay one hundred bucks an hour, that's a straw

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:51.000
man.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:52.000
Red herring.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.500
That's not an equilibrium minimum wage.

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:55.110
It's not adjusted to cost of living.

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:56.949
It's not adjusted to anything that makes sense.

00:04:56.949 --> 00:04:58.639
It's a straw man from the right.

00:04:58.639 --> 00:05:02.629
They love to say, hey, listen, people having
more money is better.

00:05:02.629 --> 00:05:06.330
Make the minimum wage 100 bucks an hour, make
it a thousand bucks an hour.

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:07.440
Nobody's calling for that.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:12.740
That's a serious person because that's not
an equilibrium wage tied to inflation, productivity,

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:13.940
cost of living or any.

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:22.909
Else that being said, remember that when people
make more money, that is a cost to the employer,

00:05:22.909 --> 00:05:29.480
but then people in the community have more
money to go and spend at that business and

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:31.759
imagine a hardware store, for example.

00:05:31.759 --> 00:05:36.240
This is often an example that's given hardware
store all of a sudden has a higher wage.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:39.950
They have to pay either because of the minimum
wage has gone up or because they need to raise

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:42.030
wages to attract workers.

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:44.680
They go from eight bucks an hour to 12 bucks
an hour.

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:46.849
Oh, their payroll costs have just gone up.

00:05:46.849 --> 00:05:47.849
That's terrible, right?

00:05:47.849 --> 00:05:48.849
That'll kill their business.

00:05:48.849 --> 00:05:50.009
Well, hold on a second.

00:05:50.009 --> 00:05:55.620
If the entire region now sees a twelve dollar
minimum wage instead of eight, you now have

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:59.819
all sorts of people who, because they are
earning more money, they're going to go to

00:05:59.819 --> 00:06:01.280
the hardware store and say, hey, you know
what?

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:06.430
I'm going to get a new vacuum or I'm going
to get what I need to refinish my floors or

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:09.199
I'm going to finish my basement or whatever
the case may be.

00:06:09.199 --> 00:06:13.759
And even though the hardware store now has
to pay more out in wages, they have all of

00:06:13.759 --> 00:06:17.070
these customers that now have money to go
and spend and do projects.

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:19.150
So revenue goes up as well.

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:24.460
This analysis only works within what we would
consider an equilibrium wage.

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:26.979
It's really important to understand that.

00:06:26.979 --> 00:06:30.449
So that's the latest in terms of unemployment
claims.

00:06:30.449 --> 00:06:33.689
This is going to be resolved mostly on its
own.

00:06:33.689 --> 00:06:35.669
Some businesses are going to have to start
paying more.

00:06:35.669 --> 00:06:39.840
There's no question about that because they
have been paying submarket wages for a really

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:44.400
long time and that is going to be a problem
for them.

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:46.749
For the most part, this issue is going to
resolve itself.

00:06:46.749 --> 00:06:49.749
I'm already seeing it happen in these numbers.

